\begin_layout Title
Do Price Freezes or Subsidies Lead to Increased Enrollment?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 10, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to the US News, 80% of the fastest growing occupations in the
 United States depend on the mastery of mathematics and scientific knowledge
 and skills.
 However, there has been a signficant shortage of qualified graduates with
 the necessary skills to occupy these positions.
 To address the issue, Florida Governor Rick Scott recently suggested that
 students majoring in science, technology, engineering, and math (STEM)
 programs be offered lower tuition than those studying in the liberal arts
 or social sciences.
 However, individuals associated with the liberal arts and social sciences
 have began protesting the possible measure claiming that tuition disparity
 is unjust.
 Instead, they suggest offering students in STEM majors additional financial
 aid subsidies to encourage enrollment in these programs, while preventing
 tuition hikes for a large group of students.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

While this is a clear debate over public policy, an appropriate experiment
 could provide a basis for an informed discussion concerning which policy
 is more effective in achieving the intended outcome.
 The experiment I have in mind consists of three stages with each being
 a simple representation of a far more complicated process found in the
 real world.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Stage 1: Subjects are given a fixed endowment.
 They are given the option to choose between completing a series of math
 problems, consisting of the four main operations of arithmetic, or a series
 of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

sliding
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 games.
 To participate in either task, the subjects must pay a fixed price, which
 will be altered by the experimenter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Stage 2: In a preset amount of time, subjects will try to complete as many
 problems as possible for their chosen task.
 There will be a total of 50 sliders and 25 math problems.
 The subjects will be told that their performance on these tasks will affect
 the parameters of the lottery they receive in the next stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Stage 3: The subjects who chose the math problems will each receive a specific
 lottery based on their performance in the previous stage
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{M}\sim U[a,b]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\mbox{\# of correct problems}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b=a+25$
\end_inset

.
 A random number will be drawn from this distribution, and this will be
 the amount of compensation they receive.
 The same setup applies to those subjects who chose the sliding games, but
 their lotteries have a slightly difference construction
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{S}\sim U\left[c,d\right]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c=\frac{\mbox{\# of properly completed sliders}}{2}-5$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $d=\mbox{\# of properly completed sliders}-10$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is important to note that all subjects will be explained the entire setup
 of the experiment so as to make the most informed decision possible in
 the first stage.
 The treatment variable of this experiment is the listed prices of the two
 tasks in the first stage.
 The first treatment of the experiment, acting as the control, will have
 subjects choosing between the two tasks when prices are equal.
 In the next treatment, the price of the math problems remains the same,
 while the price of the sliders has increased.
 Finally, the last treatment will offer a slight variation of the previous
 two.
 While the displayed prices of the two tasks will remain equal, the subjects
 will be informed that if they choose to participate in the math problems,
 they will be granted a small subsidy in addition to the compensation they
 receive at the end of each trial.
 A session will consist of running all three treatments with the same subjects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The question we are trying to answer is this: Does a price freeze or a subsidy
 encourage more individuals to choose the math problems? The theory predicts
 that both designs will lead to the same outcome.
 To illustrate this result, let 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{S}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{M}$
\end_inset

 denote the total value subjects place on the sliding games and math problems,
 respectively.
 Note that these values incorporate the unknown compensation that subjects
 receive as well as the effort they must expend to participate in either
 task.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{S}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{M}$
\end_inset

 are both random variables, and we assume they are both distributed normally
 with respective means 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{S}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{M}$
\end_inset

 and common variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 As a result,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{S}-V_{M}\sim N(\mu_{S}-\mu_{M},2\sigma^{2})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, the ex-ante expected payoff of subject 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 as a function of his chosen task is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\Pi_{i}(M) & = & V_{M}-P_{M}\\
\Pi_{i}(S) & = & V_{S}-P_{S}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{M}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{S}$
\end_inset

 are the given participation payments.
 We can now determine the probability of person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 choosing the math problems,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Pr_{i}(M) & = & P(\Pi_{i}(M)>\Pi_{i}(S))\\
 & = & P(V_{M}-P_{M}>V_{S}-P_{S})\\
 & = & P(P_{S}-P_{M}>V_{S}-V_{M})\\
 & = & P(V_{S}-V_{M}<P_{S}-P_{M})\\
 & = & P\left(\frac{V_{S}-V_{M}-\mu_{S}+\mu_{M}}{\sqrt{2}\sigma}<\frac{P_{S}-P_{M}-\mu_{S}+\mu_{M}}{\sqrt{2}\sigma}\right)\\
 & = & \Phi\left(\frac{P_{S}-P_{M}-\mu_{S}+\mu_{M}}{\sqrt{2}\sigma}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Therefore, the probability of choosing the math problems should only depend
 on the prices of the two tasks.
 If the price freeze and subsidy both lead to the same inherent price difference
 between the two tasks, then there should be no distinct change in the probabili
ty of a subject choosing the math problems.
 However, I believe there will be a greater response to the subsidy then
 the price freeze.
 In my opinion, the prospect of guaranteed additional compensation provides
 a better incentive of choosing the math problems then raising the price
 of the sliding games.
\end_layout

